git commands:

1. git config --global user.name "name"  -> To set profile name for gith
2. git config --global user.email abc@gmail.com -> here "" not required to set the email
3. git config --global init.default branch main -> initialise the branch name , here it is main, it can be anything, "" not required.
4. git config -h -> gives all the list of commands and its detailed summary.
5. git help config -> gives all the list of commands in more detailed way in a new webpage(internet not required)
6. clear -> to clear the bash screen
7. Now suppose we want to create a git for a particular folder where our files are stored, so we move into that folder in the bash using the command
	- cd c:/user/rasel/python_files 
	- git init -> now a hidden folder as git will be initialise in this file location which will keep track of any updation to any files done in this folder
	- git status -> gives what files are present in the current folder or any folder is being tracked/untracked (if a file is untracked it wont effect anything in git because)
	- git add a.txt -> to track any file in that folder
	- git add -all -> to track all the files
	- git add -A -> to track all the files
	- git add . -> to track all the files
	- git rm --cached a.txt -> to make untrack.branc
	suppose there is a very important file which we don't want others to see it in our git we can also do that
	- we create a file with extension as .gitignore
	- open it as notepad 
	- # ignore all .txt files -> ignores all .txt files
	- * .txt - > ignore all .txt files
	-  git status -> to verify if git ignored
commit:
	Its like taking a snapsot of git at current time, it's like finalizing the repository after all the changes we make
	git commit -m "My first commit" -> we can give any message 
	Now type git status -> we see a message as no files to commit since we didn't do any updation of any files

Now suppose after commit we change the content of any file outside the git folder in the same location, now to see the changes we type
	- git status -> in red mark we will see which file has been updated/modified
	- git diff -> it displays the file name which has been changed , the red text are the text before update and green are the ones after update.

Suppose we make a change in file a.txt and we want it to be added in git history books so to achieve it
	- git add a.txt -> file sits in staging
	- git restore --stagged a.txt -> remove from staging(history books)
	- git commit -a -m "done updating and committed"
	- git rm "filename" -> to delete a file from the folder
	- git restore "filename" -> to restore that file in the folder
	- git mv "a.txt" "b.txt" -> rename a file

git log -> to view all the different commits
git log --one line > to view commits in different 
git log -p -> to view all the recent commits
git rebase -i --root -> to reindex the commits as per our wish


